/* native/forge/tools/usage.inc - CommandLineParser.Usage of src/fire.Compiler/CommandLine.cs (commit b833e71b), as forge prints it (tools/commands.c) */
    "Usage:\n"
    "  fire.Compiler run   <file>... [-m DEBUG|RELEASE|PERFORMANCE] [-f 32|64] [-i 32|64] [--global-jobs safepoints|sleep|sync] [-- <arguments of the program>...]\n"
    "  fire.Compiler build <file>... [-o <target.exe>] [-m DEBUG|RELEASE|PERFORMANCE] [-f 32|64] [-i 32|64] [--engine vm|native] [-t <target>] [--toolchain <name>] [--config <file>] [--keep] [--stripping 0-3] [--keep-all] [--optimize speed|size] [--linkage source|static|shared|plugin] [--host <program>] [--split auto|on|off] [--quick[=0|1]] [--global-jobs safepoints|sleep|sync]\n"
    "  fire.Compiler publish <solution.firesln>|<project.fireproj> [-p <project>] [--toolchain <name>] [--quick[=0|1]]\n"
    "  fire.Compiler build|publish ... [--report json] [--overlay <source>=<file>]... [--debug-info] [--check] [--all]\n"
    "  fire.Compiler native <file>... [-o <target.cpp>] [-t <target>] [-f 32|64] [-i 32|64] [--config <file>] [--stripping 0-3] [--keep-all] [--optimize speed|size] [--global-jobs safepoints|sleep|sync]\n"
    "  fire.Compiler bytecode <file>... | <project.fireproj> | <solution.firesln> [-p <project>] [-o <out.fbc>] [-m ...] [-f 32|64] [-i 32|64] [-D name]... [--report json] [--overlay <source>=<file>]...\n"
    "  fire.Compiler dump  <file>... | <project.fireproj> | <program.fbc> [-o <file>] [-m ...] [-f 32|64] [-i 32|64] [-D name]...\n"
    "  fire.Compiler ui    <file.fxml> [-o <file>]\n"
    "  fire.Compiler biscuit <file.biscuit> [-o <file>]\n"
    "  fire.Compiler falstad <circuit.txt> [-o <file.biscuit>]\n"
    "  fire.Compiler model <file.gltf|file.glb> [-o <file.f3d>] [--fps n]\n"
    "  fire.Compiler stats <before.stats.json> <after.stats.json>\n"
    "  fire.Compiler run|build <project.fireproj | solution.firesln> [-p <project>] [...]\n"
    "  fire.Compiler test  [<file>... | <project.fireproj> | <solution.firesln>] [-p <project>] [--engine vm|native] [-m ...] [-f 32|64] [-i 32|64]\n"
    "  fire.Compiler data migrate|status|diff|import [<file>... | <project.fireproj>] --url <url> [...]   (forge data --help)\n"
    "\n"
    "run     compiles the files into one program and runs it. What follows `--` goes to the program (Environment.Args; for a project instead of its settings.arguments).\n"
    "build   turns them into a program (a project without -o or \"output\": <project>/bin/<target>-<mode>/<name>, target vm or the native one, mode dbg/rel/perf):\n"
    "        a self-contained executable that carries the VM (default: out.exe), or - with --engine native, or \"engine\": \"native\" in\n"
    "        fire.native.json - C (and the natives in C++) built with the toolchain of the configuration for the target (-o is the program; for a toolchain that only writes files, a folder).\n"
    "        A target other than this machine (-t esp32, wasm, another system) is always native: the VM exists only for this machine.\n"
    "-m      Execution mode (default: whatever the script sets with #debug/#performance, otherwise RELEASE).\n"
    "native  translates the files into C (written next to fire_rt.h, the runtime it includes; default: out.c - and fire_natives.cpp with natives in C++)\n"
    "        - an experimental ahead-of-time backend, see docs/spec/NATIVE_BACKEND.md.\n"
    "-t      Target of a native build: windows, linux, macos, esp32, freertos, wasm (a page for the browser, needs Emscripten) or one that fire.native.json defines (default: this machine). The target decides the\n"
    "        default precision of float, which libraries can be imported, which platform package of the runtime is used and how the program starts.\n"
    "--config  The native build configuration (default: the nearest fire.native.json next to the first file, else built-in defaults): engine, target,\n"
    "        toolchain (which C++ compiler), own targets. The editor edits the same file.\n"
    "--toolchain  The compiler of a native build (gcc, clang, msvc, tcc, or one of fire.native.json). Without it: the project's own toolchain, else the\n"
    "        compiler chosen for this machine (spark: Tools > Options > Compilers) - build and test use the Build compiler, publish the Publish compiler.\n"
    "--stripping 0-3  How much a native build leaves out of what the program does not reach (docs/spec/NATIVE_BACKEND.md; \"stripping\" in fire.native.json or the project):\n"
    "        0 none (everything stays, as in the VM), 1 protected (every class and public member stays), 2 methods (every class with its constructors, fields\n"
    "        and properties stays, a method only when it is used), 3 all (the default: only what the program reaches). Names work as well (--stripping methods).\n"
    "        Names given as literals to reflection (Reflect.New(\"Dog\")) always stay; a debug build always keeps everything.\n"
    "--keep-all  The same as --stripping 0.\n"
    "--stats  A native build writes a report next to the program (<output>.stats.txt and .json): the code by origin (the generated code per module, the runtime,\n"
    "        the C of the packages, the C library - from the linker's map), the data, the typing of the generated functions (C numbers or Values, which have a\n"
    "        C interface and why not), the biggest functions, and for DOS the memory the program needs to load.\n"
    "--optimize speed|size  The direction of a native build: the flags of the C compiler (gcc/clang -O2 or -Os with sections, msvc /O2 or /O1,\n"
    "        Watcom -Ot or -Os) and the choices of the generated code (FIRE_OPTIMIZE_SIZE). Before the project (\"optimize\") and fire.native.json;\n"
    "        by default the target's (the PC targets of Open Watcom: size, the others: speed).\n"
    "--linkage source|static|shared  How a native build links the libraries and packages: source (the default) puts everything into one C file; static builds each\n"
    "        library once as a static library in the user-wide cache (ember cache clean) and links it; shared (Linux, macOS, Windows with MinGW) builds them as shared libraries (DLLs) put next to the program (\"linkage\" in fire.native.json or the project);\n"
    "        plugin builds the classes of the program as a fire plugin (<output>.so/.dll and <output>.fireplugin for the VM) that a program with linkage shared loads with Library.Load.\n"
    "--host <program>  With --linkage plugin: the program (built with linkage shared) the plugin is for - its switches and libraries (<program>.firehost next to it).\n"
    "--split auto|on|off  The C of the program in several translation units that the compiler builds at the same time (one per core; FIRE_BUILD_JOBS), then linked:\n"
    "        auto (the default) splits a large program (2 MB of C) built with gcc, clang or Emscripten; on splits whenever the toolchain can; off keeps one file\n"
    "        (\"split\" in fire.native.json; FIRE_SPLIT=1/0 goes before both; docs/spec/NATIVE_SPLIT.md).\n"
    "--quick[=0|1]  A quick native build for tests, not for releases (build, publish; spark's hare switch): the C compiler without optimisation (-O0; --quick=1: -O1;\n"
    "        msvc /Od, /O1) and the program in units built side by side where the toolchain splits (gcc, clang, MinGW, Emscripten - forced on, also for publish).\n"
    "        The mode (debug, release, performance) stays; the natives of the packages keep their optimisation. TinyCC, Open Watcom, files and custom build as without it,\n"
    "        so do the linkages static, shared and plugin. Publish marks it: \"Schnellbuild (-O0, geteilt)\".\n"
    "-f      Precision of float in bits: 32 or 64 (default: whatever the script sets with #floatwidth, otherwise 64).\n"
    "-i      Width of int in bits: 16, 32 or 64 (default: whatever the script sets with #intwidth, otherwise 64 - also on small targets).\n"
    "--types lax|strict  How a type conflict (assigning a string to an int, a wrong argument type) is reported: a warning (lax, the default) or an error (strict). A project setting\n"
    "        typeChecking does the same, #lax / #strict in a file decides for that file (docs/spec/TYPES.md).\n"
    "--global-jobs safepoints|sleep|sync  When the main program runs the `fire global` jobs (and lets in the sections of its threads, the host callbacks) by itself:\n"
    "        safepoints (the default: at a loop's jump back, Sleep and the wait functions), sleep (only at Sleep and the wait functions) or sync (only at `sync globals`);\n"
    "        `sync globals` and the end of the program always do. Goes before the project's globalJobs, #globaljobs / #nosync in the source and \"globalJobs\" in\n"
    "        fire.native.json (docs/spec/THREADING_DESIGN.md section 7). The VM and a native build behave the same.\n"
    "--stacktrace off|calls|lines  What an exception shows of its stack: the functions with their lines (lines, the default in release), without lines (calls) or\n"
    "        nothing (off, the default in performance); debug always shows lines. Goes before #stacktrace in the source, the project's stackTrace and \"stackTrace\"\n"
    "        of the target in fire.native.json (docs/spec/STACKTRACE.md).\n"
    "--report json  build and publish write a report for tools instead of the text: one JSON object per line on stdout (progress, log, errors and warnings with\n"
    "        file, line and column, what was written, the end) - what spark reads (docs/tools/forge.md). The line \"cancel\" on the input (or SIGTERM) cancels the build.\n"
    "--overlay <source>=<file>  The text of the source file is read from <file> (an editor's unsaved text); repeatable.\n"
    "--debug-info  A native build for the debugger: without optimisation, with debug information, not stripped.\n"
    "--check  A library project is only compiled (for the target of -t), not packed.\n"
    "--all   Builds every project of the solution in the order of its references (a library is compiled, a program built; spark's Build Solution).\n"
    "-D name Defines the symbol \"name\" for #if (repeatable, also --define name or -Dname).\n"
    "-o      Name of the file produced by build or native.\n"
    "bytecode  compiles the files into one program and writes it as a program file (default: out.fbc; the format: docs/spec/BYTECODE.md, \"Das Dateiformat .fbc\").\n"
    "        With --report json (spark's Run/Debug on the VM in C): the report of build, its outputs the program file (\"program\"), the libraries of its\n"
    "        packages, built now (\"library\"), and the VM `fire` of its widths (\"vm\").\n"
    "dump    writes a program as text, everything a program file holds, made to be compared: the files compiled into one program, or a program file (.fbc);\n"
    "        without -o to the output.\n"
    "test    runs the tests: the methods with [Test] (docs/tools/testing.md), in the VM or with --engine native as a native program for this machine. Without a file: the solution or the\n"
    "        project in the current folder (all projects of a solution, or -p <name>). The files in testing/ of a project belong to its tests; the top level of a program does not run.\n"
    "        --filter A.B,C runs only these tests (Class.Method, or a class for all its tests; * for any text). Exit code 0 when every test passed, 1 otherwise.\n"
    "ui      writes the script generated from the markup of a user interface (docs/ui/markup.md) - not needed to build: `#include \"x.fxml\"` does it on the fly.\n"
    "biscuit writes the script generated from the schematic of a circuit (docs/circuits/README.md) - `#include \"x.biscuit\"` does it on the fly as well.\n"
    "falstad converts a circuit of Paul Falstad's simulator (File > Export As Text) into a schematic (.biscuit); what the package circuits has not got is named.\n"
    "\n"
    "A project (`.fireproj`) or a solution (`.firesln`, docs/tools/projects.md) instead of the files: run runs the project (of a solution: the startup project, or -p <name>), build builds it -\n"
    "a program like above (the settings of the project decide: engine, target, mode, output), a library as a package (`name-version.fpk`, in the folder of -o, the project's `output` or `bin`).\n"
    "The settings of the project go before the tags in the source; the options of the command line go before both.\n"
    "\n"
    "File names without spaces do not need quotation marks."
